                <h2>Q1: How many creatives should a new TikTok Shop product have at launch?</h2>
<p><strong>Our recommendation is clear: prepare 12–18 creatives for the first test cycle. Producing only three to five videos and scaling them immediately is usually a mistake.</strong></p>
<p>A new product has little historical conversion data, so the creative pool must test different combinations of value propositions and audiences. Start with three angles: pain-point demonstrations, before-and-after comparisons, and authentic creator reviews. For each angle, produce four to six versions with different hooks, pacing, captions, or opening shots.</p>
<p>The assets do not all need to be highly polished, but they must remain consistent with the product listing, inventory status, and promotional offer. If the video promises one price or benefit while the product page shows another, the campaign may generate clicks without purchases and increase refunds.</p>
<h2>Q2: Should the pool contain only brand-produced videos, or creator content as well?</h2>
<p><strong>Creator content is essential. However, it should not be treated as disposable, low-cost advertising material.</strong></p>
<p>Brand-produced videos are useful for explaining features, demonstrating usage, and presenting promotions. Creator videos provide a more credible consumption context and can reduce the skepticism associated with obvious advertising. For cold starts, combine in-house content with a structured creator-seeding program.</p>
<p>Before publishing creator assets, confirm usage rights, permitted channels, editing rights, and the duration of the authorization. Tag every file with the product version, production date, authorized platform, promotion details, and editing status. This prevents compliant, reusable assets from being mixed with expired or restricted content during scaling.</p>
<h2>Q3: Do we need multiple ad accounts to test a new product?</h2>
<p><strong>Usually, no. Start with one compliant account and a clear testing structure.</strong></p>
<p>Multiple accounts, payment methods, and mixed reporting structures make it difficult to identify whether performance is driven by the product, the creative, or account settings. Use the primary account for daily campaign management, while allowing agencies, subsidiaries, or creators to collaborate through official Business Center permissions where possible. This also reduces duplicate account-opening risks and unusual login activity.</p>
<p>If a multi-account structure is necessary, assign an owner, funding plan, naming convention, and reporting template to each account. Low balances, inconsistent payment information, and frequent environment changes can increase review and compliance risks. For teams managing several accounts, centralized balance alerts and top-up records help prevent campaigns from stopping abruptly while new funds are processed.</p>
<h2>Q4: Should the budget be funded once or replenished in stages?</h2>
<p><strong>Replenish it according to the testing cycle and maintain enough usable balance for several days.</strong></p>
<p>Separate the budget into a testing pool and a scaling pool. The testing pool covers initial creative evaluation, audience testing, and value-proposition validation. The scaling pool is released only to assets that have already generated consistent clicks, add-to-cart actions, or other meaningful signals.</p>
<p>Balance alerts should consider average daily spend, expected top-up processing time, and upcoming promotional periods—not just a fixed cash threshold. If an account can fund only that day’s spend, a delayed top-up may interrupt delivery exactly when a creative is beginning to show useful data.</p>
<h2>Q5: Which creatives should be paused, and which should receive more budget?</h2>
<p><strong>Do not pause an asset solely because its views are low, and do not label a high-view video as a winner without further evidence.</strong></p>
<p>Evaluate the first stage using click-through rate, landing-page engagement, and product clicks to determine whether the creative communicates the value proposition clearly. Then examine add-to-cart behavior, cost per purchase, and refund signals to determine whether the offer, price, inventory, and product page match the ad.</p>
<p>After a reasonable minimum delivery threshold, pause assets whose click-through rate remains materially below the account’s recent median. If an asset generates stable add-to-cart activity but weak conversion, review pricing, promotions, inventory, and the product page before replacing the video.</p>
<p>Do not delete underperforming assets. Record the reason—such as an overloaded first three seconds or an offer mismatch—so the next production cycle avoids repeating the same mistakes.</p>
<h2>Q6: If a creative generates substantial GMV, how do we confirm the actual profit?</h2>
<p><strong>Reconcile at least platform gross sales, ad spend, creator commissions, refunds and discounts, fulfillment costs, and payment fees.</strong></p>
<p>TikTok Shop sales figures are not the same as final distributable profit. Finance teams should build a daily reconciliation table by order, date, account, and creative. Match ad-account consumption with platform invoices, then align settlement records with the refund cycle.</p>
<p>Creator seeding, commissions, product samples, and production costs should also be included in the return-on-creative analysis. Without these costs, a high-sales video may appear profitable even when its net contribution is weak.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Cold-start success does not come from producing as many assets as possible. It comes from assigning every creative a clear testing role and tracing every result.</strong> Build a minimum viable pool of 12–18 assets, evaluate them through clicks, conversions, and net profit, and replace them systematically as performance signals emerge.</p>
</blockquote>
